  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses Through an LTI System",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the output of an LTI system for a scaled shifted impulse by applying homogeneity and time invariance to convert a $\\delta$(t - t0) into a $h(t - t0)$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses Through an LTI System",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Scaled Impulses Through an LTI System",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The object to recognize is a scaled shifted impulse entering an LTI system. Start from the impulse-response definition: if the input is the unit impulse $\\delta(t)$, then the output is the impulse response $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t)\\}=h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Homogeneity handles the coefficient. If the impulse is multiplied by a constant $a$, then the output is multiplied by the same constant. The system response changes from $h(t)$ to $a h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{a\\delta(t)\\}=a h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Time invariance handles the shift. If the unit impulse is shifted to $\\delta(t-t_0)$, then the impulse response is shifted by the same amount.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t-t_0)\\}=h(t-t_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a scaled shifted impulse, apply both ideas. The coefficient $a$ copies onto the output by homogeneity, and the shift $t_0$ copies onto $h$ by time invariance.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{a\\delta(t-t_0)\\}=a h(t-t_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the concrete action is: replace $\\delta$ with $h$, keep the same coefficient, and keep the same time shift. Do not change the impulse into a convolution integral; the LTI system is already represented by its impulse response $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses Through an LTI System",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Finding the Output for a Scaled Unshifted Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t)$. Find the output $y(t)$ when the input is $x(t)$ = 3$\\delta$(t).",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is a scalar multiple of the unshifted impulse. Since the system has impulse response $h(t)$, the output to the unit impulse $\\delta(t)$ is $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t)\\}=h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The coefficient multiplying the impulse is $3$. Because the system is linear, homogeneity says that multiplying the input by $3$ multiplies the output by the same factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{3\\delta(t)\\}=3T\\{\\delta(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the impulse-response relationship into that scaled output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{3\\delta(t)\\}=3h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the system output is $y(t)=3h(t)$. The coefficient copies onto $h(t)$, and there is no time shift because the input impulse is unshifted.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses Through an LTI System",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding the Output for a Scaled Delayed Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t)$. Find the output $y(t)$ when the input is $x(t)$ = 4$\\delta$(t - 2).",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is a scaled delayed impulse. The coefficient is $4$, and the impulse part $\\delta(t-2)$ is the unit impulse delayed by $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the impulse-response relationship: the unshifted impulse $\\delta(t)$ produces the output $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t)\\}=h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use time invariance for the delay. Since $\\delta(t-2)$ is delayed by $2$, the output is the same delay applied to the impulse response. The minus sign in the input argument stays a minus sign in the output argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t-2)\\}=h(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply homogeneity to keep the multiplier $4$ on the delayed response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{4\\delta(t-2)\\}=4h(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the system output is $y(t)=4h(t-2)$. The scalar and the delay both transfer from the impulse input to the impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses Through an LTI System",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding the Output for a Scaled Advanced Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t)$. Find the output $y(t)$ when the input is $x(t)$ = 5$\\delta$(t + 1).",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is a scaled advanced impulse. The coefficient is $5$, and the impulse part $\\delta(t+1)$ is advanced by $1$ because it can be read as $\\delta(t-(-1))$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the impulse-response relationship: the unshifted impulse $\\delta(t)$ produces the output $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t)\\}=h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use time invariance for the advance. The output receives the same shift as the input impulse, so the plus sign in $\\delta(t+1)$ stays a plus sign in $h(t+1)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t+1)\\}=h(t+1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply homogeneity to keep the multiplier $5$ on the advanced impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{5\\delta(t+1)\\}=5h(t+1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the system output is $y(t)=5h(t+1)$. The positive coefficient stays attached to the response, and the advance remains an advance.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses Through an LTI System",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Finding the Output for a Negative Scaled Shifted Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t)$. Find the output $y(t)$ when the input is $x(t)$ = -2$\\delta$(t - 3).",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is a negative scaled shifted impulse. The coefficient is $-2$, and the impulse part $\\delta(t-3)$ is delayed by $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the coefficient and the shift separate. The negative sign belongs to the amplitude multiplier $-2$; it does not change the delay inside $\\delta(t-3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the impulse-response relationship: the unshifted impulse $\\delta(t)$ produces the output $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t)\\}=h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use time invariance for the shift. The delayed impulse $\\delta(t-3)$ maps to the delayed impulse response $h(t-3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t-3)\\}=h(t-3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply homogeneity to keep the full multiplier $-2$ on that shifted response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{-2\\delta(t-3)\\}=-2h(t-3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the system output is $y(t)=-2h(t-3)$. The negative sign changes the response amplitude, not the shift direction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
